post_install() {
  cat <<'EOF'
Alis has been installed.

User configuration is canonical under ~/.config/alis/config.json.
Pacman deliberately does not modify or migrate anyone's home directory.

Fresh users: prepare the canonical Alis config before running migrations,
then start Alis with 'alis run' or its compositor-specific 'alis service enable'
and 'alis service start' commands.

Upgrading from Alis: do not enable a second service or run old migration 019.
Back up settings, reconcile user config/state, verify Niri keybinds and
complete an owner-approved single-service handoff before running 'alis migrate'.
See docs/BRAND_R01_CANONICAL_CUTOVER.md in the installed documentation.

The packaged runtime lives in:
  /usr/share/quickshell/alis
EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
  cat <<'EOF'
Alis has been upgraded.

Package-managed installs use pacman for updates. Pacman does not migrate
user home directories. If alis.service is still present, DO NOT start Alis
in parallel or run 'alis migrate' yet. Follow the owner-gated rebrand handoff.
After user config is reconciled and only alis.service owns the shell, run
'alis migrate' and then 'alis restart' as the affected user.
EOF
}

pre_remove() {
  # Optional hardware state belongs to Hadalird and survives core removal.
  :
}

post_remove() {
  cat <<'EOF'
Alis has been removed.

Pacman does not modify per-user systemd state. If an account previously ran
'alis service enable' without first running 'alis service disable', remove any
dangling compositor wants link in that account and reload its user manager:

  find "${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/systemd/user" -path '*.wants/alis.service' -type l -delete
  systemctl --user daemon-reload

For future removals, run 'alis service disable' as each affected user before
removing the package.
EOF
}
